{"wave":"intelligence","provider":"linear","initiative":"1e3d8674-6fbf-4aa8-9bee-1da0fa70d1b7","project":null,"synced_at":1790171957,"projects":[{"id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","slug":"trace-context","name":"Trace & Context","summary":"Human-selected portfolio work is explainable from durable local evidence. A maintainer can follow an attempted operation through its initiating intent, Work and Home, exact Loopflow-authored and provider-submitted context, provider activity, usage, outcom","definition":"Human-selected portfolio work is explainable from durable local evidence. A maintainer can follow an attempted operation through its initiating intent, Work and Home, exact Loopflow-authored and provider-submitted context, provider activity, usage, outcome, and observed consequence, with missing evidence stated honestly. This evidence supports execution diagnosis and justified prompting changes; it never selects priorities or grants execution authority. Intelligence owns evidence and context reliability, Infrastructure owns execution mechanics and repair, and Product owns human presentation and adoption. No remote telemetry service or opaque vendor-state reconstruction is required.","flows":{"first":null,"loop":null,"finally":null},"krs":[{"text":"Across fourteen consecutive days of real use, one supported local inspection path accounts for attempted core planning and execution operations, preserves their causal source identities, and distinguishes observed progress, intentional human waits, failures, and missing evidence. Twenty of twenty randomly selected settled agent Runs from the declared population reconstruct their required Loopflow-authored and provider-submitted context and connect initiating intent, Work where applicable, owning Home, provider activity, usage evidence, terminal result, and observed consequence without manual store joins. Required context includes exact submitted bytes and ordered component identities, sources, hashes, roles, and token accounting; absent required context fails reconstruction. Vendor-unavailable measurements remain explicitly unavailable. The readers remain usable against the long-lived migrated records, and the evidence neither substitutes successful process exit for delivered progress nor grants execution authority.","holds":false}],"initiative_ids":["1e3d8674-6fbf-4aa8-9bee-1da0fa70d1b7"],"team_ids":["3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3"]},{"id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","slug":"context","name":"Context","summary":"Everything the model sees is one information system. The authored surface and the dynamic surface—including Discord inputs, Wave memory, Work observations, and per-pass context—stay deliberate, minimal, bounded, and evidence-justified.","definition":"Everything the model sees is one information system. The authored surface and the dynamic surface—including Discord inputs, Wave memory, Work observations, and per-pass context—stay deliberate, minimal, bounded, and evidence-justified.","flows":{"first":null,"loop":null,"finally":null},"krs":[{"text":"For 30 consecutive days, every landed prompt, skill, flow, context-assembly, or chat-routing change cites the failing or costly run that motivated it and a follow-up run showing the intended behavior with no first-pass gate regression; zero uncited changes land.","holds":false},{"text":"For 20/20 sampled launches, the exact ordered provider context is reconstructable and every authored or dynamic component has a source, stable identity, content hash, token count, rendered order, and stated role; every launch for 30 consecutive days stays within its published total and per-layer budgets as Wave history and scratch grow.","holds":false},{"text":"Task intent survives handoffs: in 10/10 sampled multi-pass or restarted Tasks, a fresh agent receives the current human direction, full design, relevant Work observations, and current slice from durable worktree and launch context alone; no locally passing slice violates a recorded invariant or forbidden outcome.","holds":false},{"text":"Across at least three same-repository, same-flow, same-provider:model cohorts spanning at least two repositories and containing at least five landed runs in each adjacent 30-day window, median provider input tokens per run are lower in the later window and first-pass gate pass rate is no lower.","holds":false},{"text":"Zero-config excellence repeats: 3/3 fresh repositories reach a landed PR without context configuration, and no context knob is added or required for 30 consecutive days.","holds":false},{"text":"For seven consecutive days, every applicable shared Discord message is either folded into the owning Wave context within one cadence or remains visibly pending; zero audited launches receive an irrelevant shared message, and no Wave action requires another Wave raw transcript.","holds":false}],"initiative_ids":["1e3d8674-6fbf-4aa8-9bee-1da0fa70d1b7"],"team_ids":["3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3"]}],"items":[{"id":"5cb72e9c-a920-4441-900c-51d227b51669","identifier":"LOO-289","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-289/account-for-attempted-operations-and-their-observed-outcomes","name":"Account for attempted operations and their observed outcomes","description":"Establish the bounded population with Reliability using actual operation-entry and consequence evidence. Include attempts that never create Runs, explicit human waits, failures, retries, and missing outcomes without double-counting them. Start retention as soon as this boundary exists.  \n   **Done when:** every included attempt is represented with causal source identity and a defensible classification; counts and denominators reconcile; missingness remains visible; failures link to evidence and an owning repair disposition where one exists. Do not wait fourteen days before starting the next Task.\n\n## Accepted chapter allocation — 2026-09-23\n\nChapter 20260923T000959Z-502f011b; review 2026-10-21T00:09:59.226362+00:00. Queued in the same serial Intelligence slot after the preceding capture/population Task. Retaining evidence does not prevent selecting the next Task. No execution launch or publication occurs as part of creating this planning record.","rank":0,"completed":false,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"6e89912a-36e5-46fa-960a-fa48a7be5142","identifier":"LOO-290","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-290/make-reconstruction-usable-through-the-supported-inspection-path","name":"Make reconstruction usable through the supported inspection path","description":"Connect the captured evidence through current readers, with Product reviewing the human-facing contract. Avoid requiring manual filesystem/SQLite joins.  \n   **Done when:** retain the declared twenty-Run reconstruction sample and fourteen-day population, demonstrate diagnosis and applicable handoffs, and report every unmet acceptance condition explicitly. Repair reader/capture defects within Intelligence; route runtime failures to Infrastructure.\n\n## Accepted chapter allocation — 2026-09-23\n\nChapter 20260923T000959Z-502f011b; review 2026-10-21T00:09:59.226362+00:00. Queued in the same serial Intelligence slot after the preceding capture/population Task. Retaining evidence does not prevent selecting the next Task. No execution launch or publication occurs as part of creating this planning record.","rank":1,"completed":false,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"fb2f2d99-6019-4b09-a738-7c95a655a048","identifier":"LOO-288","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-288/capture-required-context-and-causal-evidence-at-the-actual-launch","name":"Capture required context and causal evidence at the actual launch boundary","description":"Use the baseline’s known gaps to identify affected real launch paths. Preserve exact prepared context, component provenance, initiating source, Home, applicable Work, parent Run, and provider-attempt identity. Distinguish missing required context from optional replay material and unavailable vendor measurements.  \n   **Done when:** changed paths produce inspectable local records through the configured harness; a fresh/restarted launch demonstrates current durable direction; absence or corruption of required context cannot receive a reconstructed verdict. Include a pre-provider failure case. Correct stale evidence-contract guidance with the implementation.\n\n## Accepted chapter allocation — 2026-09-23\n\nChapter 20260923T000959Z-502f011b; review 2026-10-21T00:09:59.226362+00:00. Opening; one serial Intelligence slot. No execution launch or publication occurs as part of creating this planning record.","rank":2,"completed":false,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"13634748-ddb3-4340-a7bc-4874e7661b0a","identifier":"LOO-272","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-272/replay-an-eligible-recorded-run-unattended","name":"Replay an eligible recorded run unattended","description":"Evidence: merged LOO-129/PR #1240 provides strict replay preflight and typed refusal; merged LOO-271/PR #1241 records immutable replay-safe native-Codex execution contracts and proved one real eligible completed invocation. The Project KR still has no execution proof: no recorded run has been relaunched from that contract, and the 10/10 last-month sample cannot begin until an unattended replay path exists.\\n\\nImplement the smallest native-Codex replay executor over the shipped contracts. Load only a strict-preflight-eligible ReplayContract, resolve every launch input from its content-addressed ExecutionContract and finalized Turn/conversation artifacts, materialize the recorded clean repository commit in isolated Loopflow-owned execution placement, launch unattended with the recorded replay-safe provider/model/account/config/process/argv/environment contract, and record the replay as a new trace linked to its source invocation without mutating the source record. Fail closed with existing typed refusal evidence when identity, hashes, authority, artifacts, or provider support do not match; never reconstruct from ambient current state.\\n\\nProof: the real eligible invocation produced by LOO-271 replays unattended through the built CLI, creates a complete independently inspectable result linked to the source, and leaves all source contract/artifact digests unchanged. Focused behavioral fixtures cover the real path and at least one post-preflight drift refusal. Keep Claude/OpenCode, legacy backfill, cohort statistics, context comparison/judging, historical gap-days, partial-capture recovery, SQLite-lock work, and remote telemetry out of scope. Return a metric proposal only if a reading would change the next Trace decision.","rank":3,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"18bf0002-e796-40a2-898f-0066aa9706b4","identifier":"LOO-271","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-271/record-immutable-execution-contracts-for-replay","name":"Record immutable execution contracts for replay","description":"Evidence: a 2026-08-23 audit of ten recent agent invocations found 0/10 strictly replayable. Complete direct captures retained hash-valid prompts, ordered context manifests, and normalized conversations, but all lacked target repository revision and an effective launch contract; all three sampled Codex rows also lacked an effective model.\\n\\nPersist a versioned, content-addressed pre-launch ExecutionContract from the exact value used to launch the provider. Bind the Home artifact authority, clean target repository root and commit, provider/non-empty effective model/account identity without credentials, provider binary/config identity, effective AgentConfig and ProcessConfig, sanitized argv, prompt/manifest references, and allowlisted behavior-relevant environment selectors. Fail before provider launch if this immutable contract cannot be written. When capture completes, atomically finalize the producer-owned ReplayContract index referencing the pre-launch contract and finalized Turn/conversation artifacts; incomplete captures never gain an index.\\n\\nProof: one real completed headless, worktree-only invocation produces an immutable contract that LOO-129's strict replay preflight accepts as eligible; missing fields, dirty source, unsupported authority, hash disagreement, and incomplete capture remain typed failures. No legacy backfill, ambient fallback, prompt-body duplication, replay execution, statistics, gap-day repair, partial-capture recovery, or SQLite-lock work. Coordinate the schema with LOO-129 PR sequence 1; stack on that Task only if implementation begins before its reader/schema PR merges.","rank":4,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c21ffbb3-9574-4a0b-b96a-729f96e5252e","identifier":"LOO-246","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-246/make-trace-audits-survive-persisted-capture-schemas","name":"Make trace audits survive persisted capture schemas","description":"Observed on the current long-lived ledger: lf doctor reports 1,767 capture integrity failures because persisted event artifacts contain historical usage and turn_usage variants that the current reader rejects even though the store is fully migrated. Restore trustworthy auditing at the explicit migration or normalization boundary; do not add silent defaults or parallel legacy DTOs, and preserve the current emitter vocabulary. Proof: fixtures for the exact historical variants become readable after migration, genuinely corrupt or partial captures remain separately visible, and lf doctor audits the real migrated ledger without schema-decode failures. This is the narrow evidence-backed successor to LOO-128; LOO-143 owns exact context manifests, LOO-129 owns replay, LOO-138 owns metrics, and LOO-238 owns concurrent capture locking.","rank":5,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"c6a6b5c3-eb4e-4b1e-a3ba-ecc3c9ada0af","identifier":"LOO-245","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-245/make-trace-integrity-audits-survive-persisted-capture-schemas","name":"Make trace integrity audits survive persisted capture schemas","description":"Make trace integrity audits survive persisted capture schemas\n\n<!-- loopflow-task-start:36c1153499673e1c87a1f755cf0bd34df58ecad0136fb5a3a06a4757e6013953 -->","rank":6,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"3806fada-74a8-4e9d-a254-b4a66fd4af08","identifier":"LOO-128","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-128/make-every-run-a-complete-inspectable-local-record","name":"Make every run a complete, inspectable local record","description":"User story\n\nFrom any Wave, Project, Task, or shipped PR, a user can open one local trace and answer: what intent launched this work, exactly what the model saw and did, what it cost, where a human intervened, and what outcome shipped. The answer survives process exit, branch deletion, and vendor transcript loss. No remote telemetry service is introduced.\n\nOne end-to-end record\n\n* Persist the exact provider-facing system/task prompts, ordered context manifest, normalized user/assistant/tool events, lifecycle, current command, artifacts, and replay-relevant execution contract.\n* Give control-plane Wave/Project/Task runs an explicit durable trace relationship; never join by title or guess from sparse identifiers.\n* Record provider, model, token boundaries, cost, and terminal usage for every agent-bearing launch path without estimating missing history.\n* Emit explicit escalation events when control passes to a human; keep ordinary errors and waiting distinct.\n* Link each Task PR sequence and merge outcome to the originating trace so completion, delivery, lead time, and cost per landed change are derived from evidence.\n* Repair and prevent cross-trace parent lineage corruption in the long-lived ledger.\n* Keep large append-only transcripts and artifacts on disk with durable pointers and summary dimensions in SQLite.\n\nProof\n\n`lf trace` reconstructs 20/20 randomly sampled new runs without a vendor transcript; every sampled Wave/Task row drills to the correct trace and delivered PR; one week of agent runs has complete provider/model/token/terminal coverage; intervention metrics come only from escalation events; and `lf doctor` passes the migrated long-lived ledger with zero cross-trace parents or unexplained agent-usage gaps.\n\nThis Task absorbs the narrow escalation, PR-delivery, lineage-repair, Wave-to-trace-link, and token-attribution tickets while preserving their concrete diagnoses as acceptance cases.","rank":7,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"0153a8e7-b9b7-4f16-817f-9697edd3204a","identifier":"LOO-129","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-129/replay-any-recent-run-from-its-recorded-execution-contract","name":"Replay any recent run from its recorded execution contract","description":"User story\n\nA maintainer investigating a regression can select a real historical run and reproduce its behavior unattended without reconstructing prompts, checking out files by memory, or depending on current ambient configuration.\n\nContract\n\n* Consume the complete local record produced by “Make every run a complete, inspectable local record”: exact provider prompts, ordered context manifest, model/provider choice, repository revision, Wave/Project/Task identity, conversation state, and relevant execution settings.\n* Materialize an isolated replay environment and refuse to substitute current files or mutable defaults for missing evidence.\n* Classify non-replayable runs explicitly, naming the missing or unsafe boundary rather than producing a misleading approximation.\n* Preserve the original trace and create a linked replay trace so differences in output, tokens, time, intervention, and artifacts are inspectable.\n* Support a one-command path suitable for debugging and future eval cohorts, with no remote telemetry or vendor-memory dependency.\n\nProof\n\nTen of ten sampled recent replayable runs execute unattended from their recorded contracts; intentionally incomplete legacy runs stop before provider launch with a precise reason; and the comparison view explains every changed input between original and replay.","rank":8,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9191217f-aa6f-4bb0-8846-a475c6f3f1ac","identifier":"LOO-130","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-130/absorbed-emit-explicit-human-escalation-trace-events","name":"[absorbed] Emit explicit human-escalation trace events","description":"Absorbed into “Make every run a complete, inspectable local record.” Its zero-row diagnosis and the requirement to distinguish human handoff from ordinary failure are now acceptance cases of that end-to-end trace contract.","rank":9,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"238dcd30-c7a9-4233-a09e-d65f50033910","identifier":"LOO-131","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-131/absorbed-record-pr-delivery-as-trace-evidence","name":"[absorbed] Record PR delivery as trace evidence","description":"Absorbed into “Make every run a complete, inspectable local record.” The survivor requires each serial Task PR and merge outcome to remain linked to its originating trace after branch deletion, enabling delivery, lead-time, and cost evidence.","rank":10,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"9057cbea-2ce2-4542-a92a-c72b36f19857","identifier":"LOO-132","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-132/run-timeline-and-trace-tree","name":"Run timeline and trace tree","description":"Shipped: lf runs folds the recent machine ledger into one process row; lf trace reconstructs the process tree with flow and skill boundaries, tokens, cost, provider/model, and prompt/output log paths. JSON DTOs are mirrored in Swift and covered by decoding tests. Residual work is tracked separately: control-run linkage, exact context capture, and replay.","rank":11,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"85307c79-3d59-42e1-ace9-a1c127299cc9","identifier":"LOO-133","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-133/absorbed-repair-cross-trace-parent-lineage","name":"[absorbed] Repair cross-trace parent lineage","description":"Absorbed into “Make every run a complete, inspectable local record.” The 2026-07-10 long-lived-ledger failure (three parent process IDs crossing trace boundaries and affecting 23 processes) remains an explicit integrity and lf doctor acceptance case.","rank":12,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"16c3d2dd-63c7-428a-aab8-f9d4717fb8c4","identifier":"LOO-134","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-134/absorbed-link-wave-work-to-durable-traces","name":"[absorbed] Link Wave work to durable traces","description":"Absorbed into “Make every run a complete, inspectable local record.” The survivor requires an explicit durable Wave/Project/Task run-to-trace relationship and forbids title matching or inferred joins.","rank":13,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"af1171f7-dee7-432f-83d6-a12f234319c8","identifier":"LOO-135","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-135/weekly-context-and-run-quality-review","name":"Weekly context and run quality review","description":"Folded into wave/intelligence/GOAL.md cadence: inspect one smooth, one costly, and one failed or heavily steered run; classify the first Context or Trace failure; file the smallest evidence-backed repair. Closed as a task because cadence belongs to the wave, not a standing backlog item or separate Evals project.","rank":14,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"ddce61c9-e7f0-4e36-8351-e8dca94e3bc3","identifier":"LOO-136","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-136/absorbed-close-token-attribution-gaps","name":"[absorbed] Close token attribution gaps","description":"Absorbed into “Make every run a complete, inspectable local record.” The 2026-07-10 evidence (56 agent-bearing processes, only 27 terminal usage rows) is retained as the baseline; every launch path must record provider, model, token boundaries, cost, and terminal usage without estimating history.","rank":15,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"99d2d2b9-fb84-423a-8a08-a572b8033635","identifier":"LOO-137","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-137/dogfood-outcome-proxy-landed-change-plus-human-intervention","name":"Dogfood outcome proxy: landed change plus human intervention","description":"Decision: compare like repo/flow cohorts against downstream evidence, not artifact readability or global token averages. Near-term outcome is a landed change; quality guardrails are test/gate result, retry or rework, and human intervention. Trace tasks now own the missing delivery and escalation records. A controlled bare-vendor harness remains parked.","rank":16,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d1114898-ec64-4506-aa2a-ebedb238883e","identifier":"LOO-138","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-138/show-whether-loopflow-improves-real-work-across-repos-and-releases","name":"Show whether Loopflow improves real work across repos and releases","description":"User story\n\nA product decision about a prompt, flow, or release can be made from real evidence: comparable Loopflow, Cadenza, Manabot, and Hootro work shows completion, delivery, wall time, cost, retries, and human intervention over time—without inventing a productivity score or a synthetic benchmark.\n\nScope\n\n* Define honest cohorts by repository, flow, task shape, provider/model, and landed outcome; prevent unlike work from being presented as a trend.\n* Build on the complete run record, explicit delivery evidence, and escalation events. Do not infer outcomes from process exit or create another store.\n* Provide one local query and a compact trend view for a release window, including data-coverage warnings and drill-down to the underlying traces.\n* Compare Loopflow-managed work with bare vendor loops only where matched real tasks exist; label observational evidence separately from controlled replay/eval evidence.\n* Turn any capability claim used in release notes or planning into a rerunnable query.\n\nProof\n\nA month of real multi-repo dogfood is queryable in one command; 20/20 sampled aggregate cells drill to the correct run and PR evidence; missing usage or delivery data is visible rather than silently excluded; and three release decisions cite and rerun the same cohort definition. Starts after complete-record, delivery, and escalation evidence land.","rank":17,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"6e5105ec-6056-41ad-86c6-7d02dbe28d9e","identifier":"LOO-139","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-139/local-run-events-ledger-with-trace-and-span-identity","name":"Local run_events ledger with trace and span identity","description":"Shipped through migrations 056-058: run_id is the trace, process_id is the span, parent_process_id is lineage, and terminal/skill boundaries carry cumulative usage. lf writes directly to the machine-local SQLite ledger and per-repo JSONL journal. The vocabulary and identity contract are enforced by schema constraints and lf doctor.","rank":18,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"bca80f9f-dfae-4db4-9fb7-8cd7b013b7a5","identifier":"LOO-140","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-140/remove-direction-from-the-product-model","name":"Remove direction from the product model","description":"Completed when directions were removed from runtime behavior and current readers/writers. The legacy waves.direction and runs.snapshot_direction storage columns remain inert; removing inert columns has no user behavior and is retired separately rather than carried as Trace work.","rank":19,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"300df2d1-f1e5-4e14-b7fa-8d7c23ffbeee","identifier":"LOO-141","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-141/retired-drop-inert-direction-storage-columns","name":"[retired] Drop inert direction storage columns","description":"Retired from Trace. The columns are inert schema residue with no reader or writer behavior. A future storage compaction may remove them when broader migration churn justifies it; they should not remain an active intelligence bet.","rank":20,"completed":true,"project_id":"2f8390f7-6614-426c-84a8-fa5291358691","project":"trace-context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"b42fc55f-eb1f-4687-906f-f08f15baadab","identifier":"LOO-267","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-267/make-tracked-work-the-substrate-for-end-to-end-automation","name":"Make tracked Work the substrate for end-to-end automation","description":"## Objective\n\nMake tracked Work, bounded Runs, and Task delivery a complete substrate that does not assume one long-lived owning process. Build Loopflow's Wave, Project, and Task controllers as a separate end-to-end automation layer above that substrate.\n\nThe lower layer must work equally well when commands are run by a human, a parent agent, cron, an external orchestrator, or several independent processes. The upper layer may pursue Work from beginning to end, but it does not change the meaning or ownership of the Work beneath it.\n\n## Two-layer model\n\n### Tracked Work and bounded execution\n\n* Wave, Project, and Task records hold durable planning, placement, and delivery facts.\n* `lf task prepare` and `lf project prepare` create or reuse Work without installing an autonomous controller.\n* `lf --as task:LOO-267 <skill>` and the equivalent Project/Wave forms run one bounded skill against that Work.\n* Several Task-bound Runs may overlap, including independent research Runs writing distinct files under `scratch/`.\n* One Task keeps one worktree, branch, active PR identity, and serial PR chain. Run attribution does not grant a writer lease or make one process the Task owner.\n* Explicit delivery and Flow commands do what they say without proving that an official controller ran the expected lifecycle.\n\n### End-to-end automation\n\n* Built-in Wave, Project, and Task controllers consume the Work substrate through the same public execution path.\n* `lf task run` may start that automation for existing controller-free Work.\n* `lf task resume` continues recorded controller progress after an ordinary stop.\n* `lf task restart <issue> [advice]` settles the prior controller attempt and starts a fresh kickoff/provider session from the current Task definition, complete scratch tree, branch state, review evidence, and advice.\n* Restart preserves the Task, worktree, branch, PR, and durable delivery history. It does not depend on the previous provider continuation being resumable.\n* Controller persistence may share SQLite, but it belongs to controller-owned rows keyed by Work id. Reading Work never requires joining controller state.\n\n## Architectural boundary\n\nOrganize the code so the dependency is literal:\n\n* `work/` owns Wave, Project, Task, Steer, Ask, placement, and delivery facts.\n* `controller/` owns end-to-end playheads, provider continuation, controller processes, and restart policy.\n* The execution kernel accepts assembled attribution and context; it loads neither Work nor controller state.\n* Controllers depend on Work and execution. Work and execution do not depend on controllers.\n\nRemove representations that encode a single resident process into core Task state: `TaskResident`, `phase_epoch`, the global Flow cursor/table, writer-token authority, controller fields on Work records, and lifecycle-derived delivery gates.\n\n## Design durability\n\nThe Task worktree remains the durable blackboard. Every fresh Run receives the current Task facts, recursively preloaded `scratch/`, branch/diff evidence, and relevant review artifacts. Research and design may be added piecemeal by independent processes without being collapsed into one resident transcript.\n\nKeep the full high-level design authoritative across implementation slices. A slice selects the current executable cut; it does not replace the north star. Review must reject locally passing work that introduces duplicate authorities, permanent adapters, or architectural dead ends.\n\n## Done when\n\n1. Controller-free Work supports multiple independent Task-bound Runs and recursive scratch preload.\n2. Two research Runs can execute as the same Task and preserve separately named artifacts in its one worktree.\n3. Starting, resuming, or restarting the built-in controller preserves Work and delivery identity.\n4. Restart creates a fresh kickoff basis even when the prior provider session failed, became stale, or is unavailable.\n5. Task and Project status distinguish Work truth from controller state.\n6. Explicit submit, arm, and land operations work whether pursuit was piecemeal or controller-driven.\n7. Source layout and `docs/architecture.md` make the one-way layer dependency explicit.\n8. Obsolete resident-era concepts and delivery gates are deleted rather than retained behind compatibility paths.","rank":0,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"d12b8570-6645-4420-b3b7-e4d0bc449791","identifier":"LOO-142","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-142/absorbed-evidence-led-builtin-prompt-maintenance","name":"[absorbed] Evidence-led builtin prompt maintenance","description":"Absorbed into “Make launch context inspectable, bounded, and measurably better.” The survivor retains the concrete builtin findings: repo-specific gate instructions, stale design language, repeated gate work, unconsumed survey output, repeated orientation boilerplate, and duplicated summarizers may change only when run evidence justifies it.","rank":1,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"a830f46e-ae40-4309-bdff-dd893ff2ed82","identifier":"LOO-143","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-143/make-launch-context-inspectable-bounded-and-measurably-better","name":"Make launch context inspectable, bounded, and measurably better","description":"User story\n\nBefore a run, a user can see the flow and context it intends to use. After launch, they can inspect exactly what reached the provider, understand why every component was included, and prove that a prompt or context change improved real work instead of merely adding tokens.\n\nEnd-to-end context product\n\n* Show the declared flow structure and ordered steps before execution; empirical duration and hotness remain trace evidence, not static declarations.\n* Persist the exact rendered system/task prompts and a manifest for every context asset: source layer, stable identity/path, content hash, token count, rendered order, and parent-goal role.\n* Name the instruction boundary explicitly: vendor frame, Loopflow-generated operating contract, builtin/flow text, Wave memory, repo [AGENTS.md/LOOPFLOW.md](<http://AGENTS.md/LOOPFLOW.md>), directions, and scratch.\n* Enforce evidence-backed keep/drop rules and named budgets as Wave history and scratch accumulate. Unconditional scratch or whole-directory loading must either earn its place in traces or be removed.\n* Use Loopflow and Cadenza evidence to decide what is general software doctrine versus repository taste; keep one owner for each lesson.\n* Audit builtin prompts for stale product language, repeated boilerplate, unconsumed survey output, and duplicated summarizers only when observed runs justify the change.\n* Let a user compare two launches component-by-component and tie each prompt/skill/flow edit to the motivating trace and follow-up result.\n\nProof\n\nThree fresh repositories reach a landed PR with zero context configuration; 20/20 sampled launches reconstruct their exact provider context and explain every component; context stays inside its published budget as a month of history accumulates; and every prompt/skill/flow change for one month cites a failing/costly run plus a follow-up run with no first-pass completion regression.\n\nThis Task absorbs the old area placeholders for builtin prompt text, context assembly, generated agent-file boundaries, declared flow UX, and the general-versus-taste question.","rank":2,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"a7adce84-0475-4160-88d1-24e55672295f","identifier":"LOO-144","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-144/absorbed-bound-context-assembly","name":"[absorbed] Bound context assembly","description":"Absorbed into “Make launch context inspectable, bounded, and measurably better.” The survivor owns deliberate keep/drop rules and budgets for scratch, Wave memory, docs, and directions; unconditional scratch and whole-directory loading are preserved as acceptance diagnoses.","rank":3,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"437ca0de-3b7a-4c06-94f9-b0dba58c8719","identifier":"LOO-145","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-145/absorbed-preview-declared-flow-structure","name":"[absorbed] Preview declared flow structure","description":"Absorbed into “Make launch context inspectable, bounded, and measurably better.” Its user story survives as the pre-launch side of the same product: show declared steps and structure before execution, while measured duration and hotness remain trace evidence.","rank":4,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"97edce69-3023-4e6a-822a-af917043edbf","identifier":"LOO-146","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-146/absorbed-define-the-generated-agent-file-boundary","name":"[absorbed] Define the generated agent-file boundary","description":"Absorbed into “Make launch context inspectable, bounded, and measurably better.” The survivor explicitly names vendor, generated operating contract, builtin/flow, Wave, repo agent-file, direction, and scratch layers and measures only the layers Loopflow owns.","rank":5,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null},{"id":"82ec5a79-93bf-4c29-ac41-77cf03d4fcae","identifier":"LOO-147","url":"https://linear.app/loopflow/issue/LOO-147/absorbed-decide-general-doctrine-versus-repository-taste","name":"[absorbed] Decide general doctrine versus repository taste","description":"Absorbed into “Make launch context inspectable, bounded, and measurably better.” The Loopflow/Cadenza comparison remains the proof mechanism for assigning each lesson to one owner instead of duplicating it across builtins and repo agent files.","rank":6,"completed":true,"project_id":"0f37b71f-3c7c-43f4-b809-ca2c346ca5fa","project":"context","team_id":"3871bed0-8c95-4077-ab72-728327d615b3","assignee":null}]}
